# S1. `MutexQueue`'s closing partial batch, counted into `reported` with the
# wrong sign.
#
# The first mutant here that no one tripped over. B2, B5 and F1 were each paid
# for by a wrong answer; this one came from `gate/mutant_survey.sh`, which broke
# `ring_bench` 180 ways and reported the 22 the suite ignored. Its `S` marks
# that provenance: a survey round rather than a manual one.
#
# The mutation flips the sign on the commit that runs *after* the producer loop,
# the one that publishes whatever is left staged when the records run out. On a
# fixture where the batch divides, that call commits zero records and `-=` is
# indistinguishable from `+=`. On a fixture with a remainder it puts `reported`
# below `received` by exactly the remainder. The run claims it wrote fewer
# records than the drain handed back, which is not a state the crate has.
#
# It survived because the two fixture families each held one half of what was
# needed and neither held both. `a_cramped_run_drops_and_the_drop_is_counted_
# from_the_drain` asserts `received <= reported` across every candidate, which
# catches this exactly. But it runs 256 records in batches of 32, where the
# remainder is zero and there is nothing to miscount. The 250-record fixture has
# the remainder, and asserted only `is_lossless()` and `received()`; it read
# `reported` on `TlsOverRing` alone, through `conserved()`, and left the other
# five candidates' closing count unchecked. All 18 tests passed with `reported`
# 26 low.
#
# This is B5's lesson a third time, and the sharpest instance of it yet: **a
# test that relates two quantities must run where they can differ.** B5 failed
# it by fixture (the candidate chosen had them equal), the `drain_final` gap
# failed it by dimension (the batch always divided), and this failed it by
# both at once. The assertion relating them lived on the fixture that made
# them trivially equal, and the fixture that could separate them never made the
# assertion. The 250-record fixture was itself added to close a blind spot, and
# arrived carrying a narrower one.
#
# Closed by moving `conserved()` into that fixture's own candidate loop. The
# re-probe went red with exactly one failure, the intended test.
#
# The `from` block carries the `fetch_add` line it does not mutate, and has to.
# The commit line alone is byte-identical to the in-loop commit at `:1053` except
# for indentation, and `occurrences()` matches raw substrings. So the shorter
# block sits inside the deeper-indented line and counts twice. The gate rejected
# it on that count before running anything. That is the >1-match rule doing its
# job, since the probe would otherwise have hit a site no round recorded.

id: S1
crate: ring_bench
file: src/lib.rs
round: 2026-08-29
round-failures: 1
detector: a_partial_final_batch_is_published_rather_than_abandoned

--- from
        taken += commit_batch(queue, &mut staged, capacity);
        reported.fetch_add(taken, Ordering::Relaxed);
--- to
        taken -= commit_batch(queue, &mut staged, capacity);   // PROBE ONLY — G12, was `+=`
        reported.fetch_add(taken, Ordering::Relaxed);
--- end
